Short answer: as a `rust_decimal::Decimal`, never a float:

```rust
use rust_decimal::Decimal;

// preferred — unambiguous
let amount = Decimal::new(1250, 2);        // 12.50
// or
let amount = Decimal::from_str("12.50").unwrap();
```

The rule comes from ADR-007 (May 2026, after the batch #4471 rounding incident): all money in Lantern is `rust_decimal::Decimal`, and `f64`/`f32` are forbidden for amounts — so don't do `12.50f64.into()` even if it compiles.

One caveat: my notes don't record whether Lantern wraps `Decimal` in a dedicated `Amount`/`Money` type (e.g. `Amount::from_cents(1250)` + currency). If such a wrapper exists in `lantern-domain` or similar, prefer it for the currency; the currency should come alongside as a 3-letter ISO code (`"USD"`), and `Decimal::new(1250, 2)` / `from_str("12.50")` is the safe underlying representation either way. If you have the repo checked out locally, I can take a look to confirm the exact API — just point me at the path.
